# Variant of cbebaa4b
# Dimension: D12 — 精灵主体颜色的多样性 (meta)
# Change: 每个精灵有自己的主体颜色，不同精灵颜色可能不同... → 所有精灵同色
#
# Design analysis for the D12 variant
# ==================================
# a) Concrete behavior and trace
# - Visual form: the yellow anchor stays unique, red pixels still mark the only legal
#   overlaps, and every non-anchor sprite now uses the same body color. I fix that body
#   color to blue(1) so the variant is visible in every sample instead of only within a
#   single sample.
# - Input/output effect: compared with the source task, the scattered input no longer
#   uses color diversity to separate pieces. The output still assembles the same tree by
#   translation only, but the human solver must distinguish pieces by bbox geometry and
#   red-port placement alone.
# - Local trace at the critical step:
#   original: when `_try_place` tests a candidate translation, two non-red pixels of the
#   same color may overlap because `arr == sub` is accepted; with mostly distinct piece
#   colors this is usually harmless.
#   variant: if every piece is blue, that same rule would let unrelated blue bars merge
#   and create false placements. The correct behavior is stricter: only red-on-red
#   overlap may survive; every other occupied/occupied collision is illegal.
#   difference: the solver's geometric legality check, not the search strategy, must be
#   updated.
# b) Complexity calibration
# - The search problem stays nontrivial because all pieces share one color, yet the
#   generator keeps varied bbox sizes, connector sides, and internal path layouts.
# - I do not add new transformation freedom such as rotation or reflection; otherwise the
#   removal of color cues plus extra motion freedom would over-expand ambiguity.
# - Uniqueness is still enforced by the existing backtracking assertion, so degenerate
#   same-shape/same-port sets are rejected during generation.
# c) Hidden-assumption audit
# - `_extract_pieces` only depends on non-black connected components: still valid.
# - Anchor detection relies on yellow(4): still valid because only non-anchor body colors
#   changed.
# - `total_ports`, red-count bookkeeping, and DFS branching depend only on red pixels and
#   translation: still valid.
# - `_try_place` previously depended on the soft assumption that equal non-red colors from
#   different pieces would almost never meet. That assumption is false in this variant, so
#   the conflict predicate must reject every non-red overlap.
# d) Placement legality
# - The generator still constructs the assembled target first and only scatters after the
#   assembled grid has passed a strict non-red-overlap check.
# - Tree growth and scatter padding remain unchanged, so pieces stay in-bounds, pairwise
#   separated in the input, and solver-compatible.
# e) Visual observability
# - The intended visual signature of the variant is immediate: every movable sprite is
#   blue in both input and output, while only the anchor is yellow and connectors are red.
# - No invisible ordering rule uses color ids; the solver logic depends only on geometry
#   and connector coordinates.
# f) Visual cleanliness
# - The assembled output still contains only one overlap type: paired red ports. Because
#   non-red overlap is now forbidden in the solver too, no muddy blue-on-blue fusion can
#   appear.
# g) Color-change inferability
# - The color change rule is minimal and global: "all non-anchor sprites share one body
